          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Tartarus denotes the deepest realm or condition of punitive confinement to which sinful angels are cast and held while awaiting final judgment. Christian interpretation commonly treats it as an infernal angelic prison, related to—but not always identical with—the final hell or lake of fire.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"2 Peter 2:4 — the sole New Testament use of tartaroō; sinning angels are cast into Tartarus and kept in gloomy confinement until judgment.\",\"Jude 6 — angels who abandoned their proper domain are kept in darkness under bonds for the judgment of the great day.\",\"1 Peter 3:19–20 — the “spirits in prison,” often associated with the same imprisoned angels.\",\"Genesis 6:1–4 — commonly connected with this tradition when the sinning angels are identified with the “sons of God.”\",\"Matthew 25:41 — the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels, often cited when Tartarus is related to final punishment.\",\"Revelation 20:1–3, 10 — Satan’s confinement in the abyss and eventual punishment in the lake of fire provide related, though terminologically distinct, imagery.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Tartarus is simply another name for hell generally, including the place of punishment for wicked human beings as well as demons.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Peter 2:4 is translated “cast them down to hell” in several influential English versions.\",\"Matthew 25:41 and Revelation 20:10 encourage a broad identification with final infernal punishment.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"It is specifically the prison of the angelic “Watchers” whose sin involved the daughters of men in Genesis 6, rather than a general destination for all fallen angels.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 6:1–4\",\"Jude 6–7\",\"1 Peter 3:19–20\",\"The Jewish interpretive background reflected in 1 Enoch, though 1 Enoch is not canonical in most Christian traditions.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The imprisoned angels are those who joined Satan in a primordial rebellion, without necessarily connecting their sin to Genesis 6.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Peter 2:4\",\"Matthew 25:41\",\"Revelation 12:7–9\",\"Revelation 20:1–3\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Tartarus is primarily figurative language for extreme degradation, divine restraint, and certain judgment, rather than a literal location within an underworld cosmology.\",\"basis\":[\"The verb tartaroō in 2 Peter 2:4 can be read as vivid imagery drawn from the Greek cultural environment.\",\"The emphasis of 2 Peter 2:4 and Jude 6 is custody until judgment rather than geographical description.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The term retains its strictly classical Greek sense as the mythological prison of the Titans or rebellious divine beings.\",\"basis\":[\"In Greek mythology Tartarus was the deepest underworld prison.\",\"2 Peter’s vocabulary may deliberately evoke that familiar cultural image.\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"There is a recognizable consensus about the broad meaning—punitive confinement of sinful angels awaiting judgment—but no settled consensus about the angels’ identity, the nature or location of Tartarus, or whether it is identical with the abyss, Hades, Gehenna, or the final lake of fire. The Greek term occurs in the New Testament only as the verb tartaroō in 2 Peter 2:4.\"}"
        }
      ],
